#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2152
msgid "US bathroom scales and forms almost never use stone. If you move between the UK and US, you will translate between stone↔lb and stone↔kg often. Freight and science do not use stone—keep it in the body-weight and informal UK context."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2393
msgid "US cups and fluid ounces are volumes, not masses. A “cup of flour” cannot enter mass = density × volume until you know flour’s bulk density—and that bulk density varies with packing. Prefer weighing flour."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1498
msgid "US customary pool calculators may output gallons. Convert gallons to litres only with a volume converter; do not treat gallons like litres."
msgstr "米国の慣習的なプール計算機はガロンを出力する場合があります。ガロンをリットルに変換するには、体積コンバーターを使用する必要があります。ガロンをリットルのように扱わないでください。"

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:278
msgid "US customary tablespoons are 14.8 mL and Australian tablespoons are 20 mL—do not assume every tbsp is metric 15 mL. Heaping vs level spoons dominate error more than unit math. Do not convert spoon volumes to grams without the ingredient density."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1268
msgid "US forms often want feet and remaining inches (5 ft 9 in), not a decimal foot (5.75 ft). Decimal feet are fine in surveying software but confuse humans on medical questionnaires."
msgstr "米国のフォームでは、小数点フィート (5.75 フィート) ではなく、フィートと残りのインチ (5 フィート 9 インチ) が必要になることがよくあります。小数フィートは測量ソフトウェアでは問題ありませんが、問診票では人間を混乱させます。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2167
msgid "US friends and apps expect a single pound figure. Convert UK stone-and-pounds to total pounds before sharing. 11 st 4 lb = 158 lb, not “11.4 lb.” That error is an order of magnitude wrong and alarms health apps."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2528
msgid "US mpg usually means US gallons; UK historical mpg used imperial gallons. Always note which gallon. Metric countries lean on L/100 km or km/L."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2165
msgid "US pounds-only world"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2092
msgid "US recipes may list 8 oz of chocolate or 1 lb of butter. Remember 16 oz = 1 lb. Convert pounds to grams via ounces or use a kilogram ↔ pound converter for large amounts: 1 lb ≈ 453.592 g."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2018
msgid "US retailers quote ft²; many international and commercial specs use m². Keep a project sheet with both columns. Convert the net area, then apply waste in your working unit, then convert again only if packaging demands it."
msgstr ""

#: src/components/conversion-details.tsx:432
msgid "Use {tFrom} when your instruments, drawings, or local standards already speak that unit. Prefer {tTo} when your collaborators, regulations, or tools expect that convention for {tQuantity}. Convert at a clear boundary and label every number."
msgstr "機器、図面、または地域の規格がすでにその単位を示している場合は、{tFrom} を使用します。協力者、規制、またはツールが {tQuantity} にその規則を期待する場合は、{tTo} を優先します。明確な境界で変換し、すべての数値にラベルを付けます。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:768
msgid "Use a trusted converter or exact powers of ten for SI prefixes. Reserve careful factor lookup for customary units and specialised astronomical lengths."
msgstr "信頼できるコンバータを使用するか、SI プレフィックスには正確な 10 の累乗を使用します。一般的な単位や特殊な天文長については、慎重な因子検索を行ってください。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1344
msgid "Use an area converter (or multiply by the exact square-metre to square-foot factor). Do not convert metres to feet and forget to square the scale."
msgstr "面積コンバータを使用します (または、平方メートルから平方フィートへの正確な係数を掛けます)。メートルをフィートに変換したり、スケールを二乗することを忘れたりしないでください。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:310
msgid "Use Celsius for weather, cooking, and most international engineering communication. Use Fahrenheit where local practice and equipment displays require it. Use Kelvin for scientific equations, cryogenics, and any context where absolute temperature matters."
msgstr "天気、料理、およびほとんどの国際的なエンジニアリング通信には摂氏を使用します。現地の練習や機器の表示に華氏が必要な場合は、華氏を使用してください。ケルビンは、科学方程式、極低温学、および絶対温度が重要となるあらゆる状況に使用します。"

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:73
msgid "Use centimetres to feet for body height, fabric length, and small room clearances written in cm on clothing or furniture sites but discussed in feet. Parents tracking child height and interior designers switching between EU and US catalogs use this. There are 30.48 cm in one international foot."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:19
msgid "Use centimetres to inches when you read a metric product size—screen diagonal, waist measurement, or furniture width—and need the inch figure printed on US packaging or clothing tags. Tailors, online shoppers, and DIY buyers hit this daily. One inch is exactly 2.54 cm, so the conversion is a fixed scale factor with no temperature or material correction."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2365
msgid "Use density = mass / volume with consistent weight and volume units, convert kilograms, grams, litres, and cubic metres, and avoid cup-vs-gram mistakes."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2303
msgid "Use duration converters for spans. Use time-zone tools or explicit UTC offsets for wall-clock labels. This guide trains you to pick the right tool."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1035
msgid "Use exact definitions: 1 in = 25.4 mm. Convert with full precision, then round once for the drawing. Intermediate rounding in a multi-step spreadsheet is a common source of mysterious 0.01 mm disagreements between vendors. Lock conversion cells as formulas, not pasted values, when possible."
msgstr "正確な定義を使用してください: 1 インチ = 25.4 mm。完全な精度で変換し、描画のために 1 回丸めます。複数ステップのスプレッドシートの中間丸めは、ベンダー間の不可解な 0.01 mm の相違の一般的な原因です。可能であれば、変換セルを貼り付けた値ではなく数式としてロックします。"

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:55
msgid "Use feet to metres when US real-estate listings, lumber lengths, or flight levels described in feet must feed metric planning tools, EU building codes, or GPS apps set to metres. Survey notes and climbing guidebooks also flip between these. Divide feet by 3.28084, or multiply by 0.3048 exactly."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2418
msgid "Use kilogram/gram and litre/cubic metre converters to sanitize inputs; look up density from a material datasheet, not from wishful arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2568
msgid "Use kilometre ↔ mile and km/h ↔ mph pages to clean trip data, and litre ↔ cubic metre when bulk fuel deliveries show up in m³. Apply a dedicated L/100 km ↔ mpg formula when you need the economy number itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:557
msgid "Use kilometres for distances where metres would produce thousands of units—typically travel, running routes, and geographic scales. Keep metres for buildings, rooms, and short outdoor segments."
msgstr "通常、移動、走行ルート、地理的スケールなど、メートルで数千単位が生成される距離にはキロメートルを使用します。建物、部屋、屋外の短い部分のメーターを保管してください。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2493
msgid "Use km/h ↔ mph converters for the speed side and kilometre ↔ mile for distance; apply the 60/pace bridge for the reciprocal relationship."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2260
msgid "Use knot ↔ km/h and m/s ↔ km/h tools to translate forecasts and stories, then return to knots for cockpit flows if that is your training standard."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:37
msgid "Use millimetres to inches for precision hardware: bolt diameters, PCB hole spacing, camera sensor sizes, and machining tolerances. Engineering drawings in ISO countries list mm; US tooling and drill charts are often in inches. At this scale, the exact 25.4 mm per inch factor matters more than casual rounding."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2118
msgid "Use our gram ↔ ounce and kilogram ↔ pound converters to rebuild a US recipe into metric mise en place before the oven preheats."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2343
msgid "Use our hour ↔ minute ↔ second pages for the span half of the problem; keep a trustworthy world-clock for the label half."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1664
msgid "Use our kilogram ↔ pound tools for the mass side and cubic metre ↔ litre tools when you reason about cube. Then apply the carrier formula—converters handle units; tariffs handle money."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1818
msgid "Use our knot ↔ km/h and knot ↔ mph pages for exact tables, and the nautical mile ↔ kilometre page when converting chart distances for mixed crews."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1885
msgid "Use our second ↔ minute ↔ hour tools for clean steps, and keep a notepad line with units on every intermediate value when the calculation feeds payroll, dosing intervals, or race results."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2035
msgid "Use our square metre ↔ square foot converter for the area step and metre ↔ foot for checking individual wall lengths on mixed drawings."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2193
msgid "Use our stone ↔ kilogram and stone ↔ pound pages when you need exact splits instead of guessing leftover pounds."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1630
msgid "Use outer dimensions, including bulges and protruding handles. Soft packs are measured as the rectangular envelope they occupy. Convert all three sides into one length unit before multiplying. Mixing centimetres and inches in the same product is a classic quote killer."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:173
msgid "Use square feet to square metres for EU building permits, metric tile boxes, and international property comparisons when the plan started in US customary units. Architects exporting CAD from imperial templates need m². Divide sq ft by 10.7639, or multiply by 0.092903."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:197
msgid "Use square miles to square kilometres when US state or county areas must appear in metric atlases and climate datasets. Multiply sq mi by about 2.58999. Journalists localizing US stories for global readers need this pair."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1004
msgid "Use the cup standard assumed by the recipe’s author, or convert the recipe to millilitres and grams. Metric cups (250 mL) and US cups (~240 mL) are not interchangeable for precision baking."
msgstr "レシピ作成者が想定しているカップ規格を使用するか、レシピをミリリットルとグラムに変換します。メートルカップ (250 mL) と米国カップ (~240 mL) は、精密なベーキングのために互換性がありません。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1739
msgid "Use the mile-per-hour ↔ kilometre-per-hour converter for exact values when a work zone lists an unusual number. Anchors are for glance checks; exact math is for planning and disputes."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:109
msgid "Use yards to feet for American football, fabric spreads, and garden bed lengths when the quote is in yards but the tape measure reads feet. Three international feet make one yard exactly. Contractors pacing a site in yards often switch to feet for cut accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:91
msgid "Use yards to metres when US fabric, turf, or concrete “by the yard” quotes must match metric project plans. Quilters and civil estimators working with mixed suppliers rely on this. One international yard is exactly 0.9144 m."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1713
msgid "Useful pairs to memorize for signs: 30 mph ≈ 48 km/h (urban); 50 mph ≈ 80 km/h; 60 mph ≈ 97 km/h; 70 mph ≈ 113 km/h; 80 mph ≈ 129 km/h. In the other direction: 50 km/h ≈ 31 mph; 80 km/h ≈ 50 mph; 100 km/h ≈ 62 mph; 120 km/h ≈ 75 mph; 130 km/h ≈ 81 mph."
msgstr ""

#: src/routes/$lang/terms-of-service.tsx:116
msgid "User Feedback and Conduct"
msgstr "ユーザーのフィードバックと行動"

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:186
msgid "Using “2.5 acres per hectare” is close but high by about 1%. Confusing hectares with square kilometres (100 ha = 1 km²) blows land deals by two orders of magnitude. Pasture stocking rates change with area unit—convert area before applying head-per-acre rules."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:50
msgid "Using “3 feet per metre” undershoots by nearly 10%. Ceiling heights listed as 2.4 m are about 7.87 ft, not 8 ft—important for ladder and furniture clearance. Do not mix statute miles (for long distance) into short metre-to-foot room math."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:378
msgid "Using ÷1.6 is fine for rough driving; for calibration use the exact factor. Wind forecasts in km/h vs mph change perceived severity—convert before comparing to Beaufort mental models. Do not convert pace min/km with a pure speed formula without inverting correctly."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:210
msgid "Using ÷2.54 on an area value is wrong—that is a length factor. Skin lesion charts sometimes mix mm² and cm²; convert to one unit before switching to in². Do not confuse cm² with cc (cm³) used for volume."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:426
msgid "Using ×2.2 is rough. Mixing m/s wind with mph storm reports without conversion makes Category talk inconsistent. Vertical speed in m/s (sink rate) is not the same conversation as horizontal mph."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:174
msgid "Using 0.1 m² per sq ft overstates area. Office “usable” vs “rentable” sq ft still convert with the same factor—definitions differ, units do not. Do not convert linear feet of baseboard as if they were square feet."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:402
msgid "Using 1.5 ft/s per mph is close; precision work wants 22/15. Free-fall “fps” tables are not vehicle mph conversions—context matters. Do not confuse ft/s with ft/min used in some conveyor specs."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:62
msgid "Using 1.6 as a permanent factor is fine for rough estimates but drifts on long routes; prefer 1.609344 for odometer math. Do not confuse nautical miles (1.852 km) with statute/international miles. Speed limits in km/h are not the same numbers as mph after conversion."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:440
msgid "Using 100 seconds per minute is a decimal-time fantasy, not civil time. Decimal minutes (1.5 min) are 90 s, not 1 min 5 s. Pace notation 4:30 per km is minutes:seconds, already partly in seconds—parse before converting whole minutes only."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:110
msgid "Using 2 or 4 feet per yard is a quick mental error. Square yards and cubic yards are not converted by multiplying by 3—that factor is only for linear yards. Keep partial yards (2.5 yd = 7.5 ft) as decimals."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1587
msgid "Using 2.2 as if it were exact for legal or shipping work. It is a handy approximation, not the definition. Multiplying by 2.2 and also adding a “safety pound” without knowing which scale unit you started from compounds error."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:304
msgid "Using 2.2 lb/kg is fine for luggage estimates but drifts on commercial invoices. Do not confuse avoirdupois pounds with troy pounds used for precious metals. Force in pounds-force vs mass in pounds is a physics trap—this conversion is mass to mass."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:260
msgid "Using 236.59 mL (US customary cup) vs 240 mL (US legal cup) vs 250 mL (metric cup) without matching the recipe’s origin. Thick batters measured in a liquid cup still need leveling technique. Do not convert mL of flour to cups and then to grams with a single universal factor."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:142
msgid "Using 273 instead of 273.15 is fine for rough work but wrong for precise calorimetry. Multiplying by 273.15 instead of adding is a frequent calculator error. Kelvin is never written with a degree symbol in modern SI (write 300 K, not 300 °K)."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:316
msgid "Using 30 g per ounce is a rough kitchen shortcut (~6% high). Fluid ounces measure volume, not weight—do not convert fl oz with this mass pair. Troy ounces for silver and gold differ from avoirdupois ounces."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:266
msgid "Using 30 L per ft³ is a rough estimate (~6% high). Shipping “dimensional weight” in ft³ is not the same as liquid litres of capacity—confirm whether the spec is internal volume. Natural gas ccf is hundred cubic feet of gas, not liquid litres."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:284
msgid "Using 4 teaspoons per tablespoon (an older US folk rule for some ingredients) breaks metric recipes. Liquid medicines may use a “teaspoon” marking that is not exactly 5 mL—prefer the mL scale on the syringe. Salt and spices packed tightly change mass a lot even when volume conversion is perfect."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:222
msgid "Using 4,000 m² per acre is a rough cut that drifts ~1.2%. Acre-feet measure water volume, not area—do not drop the “feet” and convert as pure acres. City lots listed in fractions of an acre need full decimal acres before multiplying."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:476
msgid "Using 5-day workweeks when the context was calendar weeks. Pregnancy “weeks” still use 7-day weeks, but trimester boundaries are medical conventions on top. Do not mix fortnight (2 weeks) without doubling."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:500
msgid "Using 60×60 incorrectly as 3,000 or 36,000 from zero slips. Decimal hours (0.5 h = 1,800 s) differ from HH:MM:SS parsing. Leap seconds rarely matter for app timeouts under a day."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:470
msgid "Using 8-hour “workdays” by accident when the context was continuous hours. 36 hours is 1.5 days, not 1 day 36 hours written carelessly. Keep UTC hour counts aligned if the day boundary matters legally."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1140
msgid "Using converters as a scale gym"
msgstr "コンバーターをスケールジムとして使用する"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:688
msgid "Using converters wisely"
msgstr "コンバータを賢く使用する"

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:414
msgid "Using the statute-mile factor 1.609 instead of 1.852 understates maritime speed. Airspeed indicated in knots is not ground speed—wind correction is separate. Do not treat knot as a land mph synonym."
msgstr ""

#: src/routes/$lang/index.tsx:138
msgid "View all guides"
msgstr "すべてのガイドを見る"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:742
msgid "Visible light spans roughly 400–700 nm. Specifying a laser as 532 nm is clearer than 5.32 × 10⁻⁷ m. Thin-film coatings and diffraction problems live naturally in nanometres. Students who only ever see scientific notation often mis-count zeros when translating to nanometres—practice both forms side by side."
msgstr "可視光の範囲はおよそ 400 ～ 700 nm です。レーザーを 532 nm に指定すると、5.32 × 10-7 m よりも鮮明になります。薄膜コーティングと回折の問題は、ナノメートル単位で自然に発生します。科学表記法しか見たことのない生徒は、ナノメートルに換算するときにゼロの数を間違えることがよくあります。両方の形式を並べて練習してください。"

#: src/lib/units.ts:1306
msgid "Volume"
msgstr "体積"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:75
msgid "Volume and area in kitchens and on land"
msgstr "キッチンと陸上の体積と面積"

#: src/routes/$lang/category.$quantity.tsx:35
msgid "Volume covers capacity and three-dimensional space—litres in the kitchen, cubic metres in construction and pools, and fine scientific units. Convert carefully when recipes or chemical charts mix cups, millilitres, and cubic metres."
msgstr "体積には、キッチンのリットル、建築物やプールの立方メートル、細かい科学単位など、容量と 3 次元空間が含まれます。レシピや化学チャートでカップ、ミリリットル、立方メートルが混在している場合は、慎重に変換してください。"

#: src/routes/$lang/category.$quantity.tsx:35
#~ msgid "Volume covers capacity and three-dimensional space—litres in the kitchen, cubic metres in construction, and fine scientific units. Convert carefully when recipes mix cups and millilitres."
#~ msgstr "体積は、キッチンのリットル、建築物の立方メートル、細かい科学単位など、容量と 3 次元空間をカバーします。レシピでカップとミリリットルが混在している場合は、慎重に換算してください。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1631
msgid "Volume for shipping math is usually the rectangular product L × W × H, not the true geometric volume of an irregular shape. Carriers bill the rectangular cuboid. Convert that cuboid into the unit the divisor expects (often cm³ or in³), then apply the formula."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:62
msgid "Volume is messier. A US cup, pint, and gallon differ from historical UK imperial measures. A US liquid gallon is about 3.785 L; an imperial gallon is about 4.546 L. Recipes that say cup without specifying the country can silently change batter hydration and cake structure."
msgstr "ボリュームはさらに厄介です。米国のカップ、パイント、ガロンは、歴史的な英国帝国の単位とは異なります。米国の液体ガロンは約 3.785 L です。 1 英ガロンは約 4.546 L です。国を指定せずに「カップ」と記載されているレシピでは、生地の水和やケーキの構造が黙って変更される可能性があります。"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2556
msgid "Volume units at the pump"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1648
msgid "Volumetric litres and cubic metres help warehouse planning (1 m³ = 1,000 L) but do not replace the carrier’s dimensional-weight formula. Use volume converters for storage and cube utilization; use the tariff formula for billing weight."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:1317
msgid "Waste factor and packaging"
msgstr "廃棄物要因と梱包"

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2025
msgid "Waste factors of 5–15% are common; patterned carpet can need more for matching. Apply waste after you trust net area. Converting units does not replace waste judgment."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2023
msgid "Waste, pattern match, and roll width"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/guides/data/guides.ts:2116
msgid "Water and many thin liquids have density near 1 g/mL, so millilitres and grams are roughly interchangeable for rough work. Oils, honey, and packed brown sugar are not. When accuracy matters, weigh."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/convert/data/conversion-pair-content.ts:143
msgid "Water boiling at 100 °C is 373.15 K in steam-table lookups. A lab bath at −40 °C is 233.15 K—below the freezing point of mercury, relevant for some thermometer discussions."
msgstr ""

#: src/routes/$lang/privacy-policy.tsx:90
msgid "We aim to collect only what we need to run and improve the site:"
msgstr "私たちは、サイトの運営と改善に必要なものだけを収集することを目指しています。"

#: src/routes/$lang/contact.tsx:109
msgid "We aim to reply within 2 business days."
msgstr ""

